Theories of transgender experience and gender identity remain largely divided between two competing frameworks. The prevailing popular and clinical discourse relies on biological essentialism, depicting gender identity as an innate, fixed essence established before social learning @APA2015Guidelines@NHS2022Gender. 
In contrast, post-structuralist queer theory treats gender as an performative construction generated by discourse, cultural norms, and power relations @Butler1990Gender. 
While post-structuralist models capture the cultural contingency of gender, they typically set aside the physical foundation of cognition, leaving open the question of how external norms become automatic bodily experience.

This theoretical divide creates an acute challenge for transgender people who are committed to scientific naturalism and philosophical physicalism. As an evolutionary biologist who identifies as gender-fluid, I experience my gender identity as immediate and deeply felt. Yet the conventional "born this way" narrative presents serious conceptual and biological difficulties. Natural selection builds flexible learning architectures and behavioural plasticity; it does not hardwire abstract, historically contingent cultural roles into prenatal brain anatomy. For an individual to identify with a gender category, they must first encounter and learn the social meanings attached to that category within their environment. At the same time, rejecting biological essentialism does not require abandoning empirical realism or physicalism. What is required is a naturalistic account capable of explaining how cultural categories become physically inscribed into the nervous system.

Predictive processing model provide an explanatory bridge across this divide @Clark2013Whatever@Veissi_re_2019@albarracin2022enacting. Under the predictive processing framework, the brain functions as a inference engine that maintains a self-model, updating its predictions to minimise sensory and social prediction errors. Through sustained immersion in cultural practices, humans infer what others expect, how they categorize actions, and which norms they share. Within the TTOM model, these shared expectations function as culturally acquired priors that selectively guide attention and behavior. When cultural priors link particular bodily features, social conventions, or names with gender categories, the brain updates its self-model to resolve ongoing prediction errors. A cognitive process can thus be socially acquired yet operate automatically and pre-reflectively, producing a psychological sense of self that feels intuitively immediate.

To examine how this happens in practice, this study presents an autobiographical case study of my own gender experience. I explore my gender identity from a rational and materialist perspective. The inquiry begins with my personal history, in which formative childhood experiences provided the foundation for a fluid or non-binary identity. As an evolutionary biologist, my scientific commitments have shaped the analytical approach I bring to understanding my own gender experience.


